course: dictionary
lesson: 11943
title: Capitalise: letters and advantage
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To capitalise a word means to write it with a capital letter, such as A, B or C, at the start. We capitalise names, places, days and months. «Maya went to London on Monday.» Maya, London and Monday all start with capitals.
explain: Capitalise is a verb, so it changes its ending: capitalise, capitalises, capitalised, capitalising. «Ben capitalised the first letter of his story» means he wrote that first letter as a capital.
explain: Capitalise has a second meaning, to use a chance to get an advantage. «The fox capitalised on the hen's mistake and grabbed the egg.» The fox did not make the mistake. It used the mistake to get something good for itself.
explain: Use the context to decide which meaning fits. If the sentence is about letters, names or words, it means to write a capital. If it is about a mistake, a chance or a team winning, it means to use it to your advantage.
fact: To capitalise means to write a letter as a capital, or to start a word with a capital letter.
fact: Capitalise on means to make the most of a chance or someone else's mistake.
fact: We always capitalise names, places, days, months and «I», even in the middle of a sentence.
fact: Money meaning: to capitalise a business is to give it money, called capital, to help it grow.
fact: A good check is to ask whether the sentence is about letters or about getting an advantage, then swap your meaning into the sentence.
exercise: «Sam capitalised the first letter of every sentence in his story.» What does capitalised mean here? | Crossed out the words | Wrote in pencil | Read the story aloud | *Wrote with a capital letter
exercise: «The team capitalised on the other side's mistake and scored.» What does capitalised on mean? | Forgot the rules | Asked the referee for help | Said sorry to the other team | *Used the mistake to get an advantage
exercise: Which word should start with a capital letter? «My friend lives in Spain.» | my | *Spain | lives | friend
test: Which word should start with a capital letter? «the bus went to school in june.» | school | in | *june | bus
test: «The shop capitalised on the sunny weather and sold lots of ice cream.» What does capitalised on mean? | *Used the sunny weather to its advantage | Painted the shop bright colours | Closed the shop early because of the sun | Wrote a sign in capital letters
exam: Which sentence is written correctly? | i went to France on friday. | i went to france on friday. | *I went to France on Friday. | I went to france on friday.
